[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. h4 g4 5. Ne5 { Authored anchor. Nf3 prevents an immediate queen check on h4. Black uses ...g5 to keep f4, so h4 attacks the supporting chain. After ...g4 the knight must move; Ne5 takes an active central post. } 5... Nf6 6. d4 d6 7. Nd3 Nxe4 8. Bxf4 Nc6 9. Nc3 f5 10. Be2 Bg7 { Plan: Develop Bc4 or d4 according to Black's reply, then solve king safety. The h-file may open for either side; do not assume the rook alone creates an attack. Opponent resource: Black can play ...Nf6 against e4 and ...d6 against Ne5. Count the defenders of e4 before chasing f7. } *

[Event "KG-02 Kieseritzky: the developing ...Nf6"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. h4 g4 5. Ne5 Nf6 6. d4 d6 7. Nd3 Nxe4 8. Bxf4 { Authored anchor. The retreat Nd3 keeps the knight protected and allows the bishop to recover f4. Black takes e4, so material recovery alone has not won the opening. } 8... Qe7 9. Be2 Nc6 10. Nc3 Nxc3 { Plan: Finish development and compare the open central files. Look for useful queen or knight pressure against the advanced e4 knight without overlooking a check. Opponent resource: Black's knight is active and your king remains central. A speculative attack on f7 can lose time against ...Bg7 and ...O-O. } *

[Event "KG-03 Kieseritzky: an immediate ...d6"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. h4 g4 5. Ne5 d6 6. Nxg4 { Authored anchor. Black attacks Ne5 with the d-pawn, but the knight can capture the advanced g4 pawn while escaping. This concrete resource is stronger than mechanically retreating to d3 in this exact position. } 6... Nc6 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 9. d4 Bh6 10. Nd5 Qxd4 { Plan: Develop the bishops and build the center while checking whether the knight will be attacked again. Compare Black's bishop development with attempts to preserve f4. Opponent resource: A similar position with an extra black developing move can change the tactics. Do not treat Nxg4 as universal whenever ...d6 attacks Ne5. } *

[Event "KG-04 Black reinforces with ...h6"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. h4 h6 5. hxg5 { Authored anchor. White captures on g5 immediately, testing the supporting pawn chain before building d4. The exact recapture matters because the h-file and rook on h8 can become tactically relevant. } 5... Bg7 6. gxh6 Rxh6 7. Rxh6 Bxh6 8. d4 d6 9. Bd2 Bg4 10. Nc3 Nc6 { Plan: Follow the printed continuation and finish development. Recount material after the whole exchange rather than assuming every pawn contact has an automatic recapture. Opponent resource: Black may develop with ...Bg7 or use the center. A careless capture can open a rook line against an undeveloped position. } *

[Event "KG-05 Black develops the bishop early"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. h4 Bg7 5. hxg5 h6 6. gxh6 { Authored anchor. White's h-pawn captures on g5 and then on h6, removing two supporting pawns. The resulting position requires exact material accounting and a development plan. } 6... Rxh6 7. Rxh6 Bxh6 8. Nc3 d6 9. Qe2 Nc6 10. d4 Bg4 { Plan: Develop the center and bishops, then choose king safety. Compare the pressure on the advanced h6 pawn with the usefulness of opening the h-file. Opponent resource: Black's g7 bishop and f4 pawn remain active factors. A pawn on h6 is not itself a mating attack. } *

