{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. Nc3 { Authored anchor. Nc3 develops instead of immediately challenging g5. It protects e4 but permits Black to expand; this is an optional comparison, not the main h4 repertoire. } 4... g4 5. Ne5 d5 6. Nxd5 Qh4+ 7. Ke2 f3+ 8. gxf3 gxf3+ 9. Kd3 Qh5 10. Nxf3 Bg4 { Plan: Meet threats to Nf3 concretely and use d4 when supported. Compare the resulting position with the h4 lines before choosing this alternative for real games. Opponent resource: A quick ...g4 gains a tempo against the knight. Quiet development does not make the advanced pawn chain disappear. } *

[Event "KG-07 Fischer Defense: build the center"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 d6 4. d4 g5 5. h4 g4 6. Ng1 { Authored anchor. Black's ...d6 controls e5, so the Kieseritzky knight jump is unavailable. Ng1 is a deliberate retreat preserving the knight after the g-pawn advance. } 6... Qf6 7. Nc3 Ne7 8. Nge2 Bh6 9. Qd2 Nbc6 10. g3 Bg7 { Plan: Use the broad center and develop the bishops. The knight must return to play, often through e2 or h3 according to the concrete line. Opponent resource: Playing Ne5 as if ...d6 were absent simply leaves the knight vulnerable. Black also threatens to complete development while you spend another knight tempo. } *

[Event "KG-08 Fischer Defense: Black develops first"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 d6 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Be7 6. Bxf4 O-O { Authored anchor. Black returns the extra pawn to develop smoothly. White obtains a center and normal pieces, but should not expect a forced attack. } 7. Bd3 Nc6 8. a3 Bg4 9. Be3 Bh5 10. O-O Ng4 { Plan: Choose Bd3 or Be2, castle and reinforce e4. A central break may be more useful than an early kingside pawn storm. Opponent resource: Black can challenge the center with ...d5 or ...c5. An undefended e4 pawn can become the target of ...Nxe4 tactics. } *

[Event "KG-09 Modern Defense: ...d5 at once"]
[Site "Original study material"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White study side"]
[Black "Black study side"]
[Result "*"]

{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 d5 4. exd5 Nf6 5. d4 Nxd5 6. Bc4 { Authored anchor. Black exchanges central pawns and develops toward the returned material. Bc4 creates pressure while White builds a useful d4 center. } 6... Be6 7. Bxd5 Qxd5 8. O-O Nc6 9. Bxf4 O-O-O 10. Be3 Qh5 { Plan: Castle, develop Nc3 and decide whether the f4 pawn is worth a separate tempo. A lead in development is valuable only if the pieces have targets. Opponent resource: Black can exchange pieces and neutralize the initiative. Do not sacrifice more material merely because the opening began as a gambit. } *

