{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 h6 4. d4 g5 5. h4 { Authored anchor. Black prepares to hold f4 with ...g5. White places d4 before challenging the supporting pawns. This reaches familiar themes by a different order. } 5... Bg7 6. hxg5 hxg5 7. Rxh8 Bxh8 8. g3 Nc6 9. Nc3 g4 10. Ne5 f3 { Plan: Follow the h-file exchanges carefully and use the center to keep pieces active. Identify whether e5 remains available to your knight before responding to ...g4. Opponent resource: ...d6 changes that availability; ...Nf6 changes the pressure on e4. Do not memorize the knight jump independently of those moves. } *

