{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 d5 3. exd5 exf4 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. d4 Nxd5 6. Bc4 { Authored anchor. Capturing f4 after ...d5 produces the same type of position as the Modern Defense. The move order differs, but the central exchanges and piece tasks converge. } 6... Be6 7. Qe2 Bb4+ 8. c3 Be7 9. O-O O-O 10. Bxd5 Bxd5 { Plan: Use the Modern Defense plan: development, king safety and pressure on active targets. Link the two routes on your repertoire map to reduce memorization. Opponent resource: An extra tempo or a different knight square can break a transposition. Compare the actual board before importing a memorized continuation. } *

