{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 f5 3. exf5 { Authored anchor. Capturing e5 is not the only way to respond to Black's central pawn action. exf5 exchanges the advanced f-pawn and leaves a concrete position to calculate. } 3... Qe7 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. fxe5 Nxe5 7. Nxe5 Qxe5+ 8. Qe2 d6 9. Qxe5+ dxe5 10. Bb5+ c6 { Plan: Develop Nf3 and choose d4 when safe. Study Black's checks and central captures before treating the extra pawn as secure. Opponent resource: The exposed kings and open diagonals make routine moves unreliable. A queen check can be more urgent than recovering another pawn. } *

