{ Original King's Gambit course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Bc4 Qh4+ 4. Kf1 { Authored anchor. Bc4 develops before Nf3 and therefore permits the queen check. Kf1 preserves the center but gives up castling. This is an alternative to learn after the main repertoire. } 4... d5 5. Bxd5 Nf6 6. Nf3 Qh6 7. d4 Nxd5 8. exd5 Bd6 9. Qe2+ Kd8 10. Ne5 Rf8 { Plan: Develop Nf3 with a tempo against the queen if the position allows, then build d4 and coordinate the rook manually. Opponent resource: An exposed king can be attacked through the center even if the queen retreats. Do not equate a queen tempo with complete compensation. } *

